# SCAN — CHANGED 2026-08-10, per explicit instruction: back in /etc/hosts, but now
# ALL {{ scan_ips | length }} IPs (was a single-IP "bootstrap fallback" entry,
# which is what caused PRVG-11368/PRVG-11826/PRVF-4664 — see docs/known-risks.md
# #28 for the full incident). Listing all {{ scan_ips | length }} here, sourced
# from the same scan_ips list dns_bind serves, means default /etc/nsswitch.conf
# order (files before dns) now returns the full, correct IP set either way — cluvfy
# only checks IP-set count/consistency, not resolution mechanism, so this satisfies
# it the same as pure DNS would. One real, known tradeoff: unlike DNS, Linux does
# NOT round-robin multiple /etc/hosts entries for the same name by default (glibc
# returns them in file order every time) — matters less than it sounds in practice,
# since SCAN's actual client load-balancing happens at the SCAN listener protocol
# layer once a connection reaches any of the {{ scan_ips | length }} listeners, not
# purely from which IP a client's resolver picked first. Kept BIND running as the
# source of truth either way (both are templated from the same scan_ips list, so
# they can't drift out of sync with each other) — if cluvfy is still unhappy with
# both in place, that'll point at something other than IP-set consistency.
{% for ip in scan_ips %}
{{ ip }}       {{ scan_name }}.{{ domain }}   {{ scan_name }}
{% endfor %}

